On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 01:26:06 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> While we're at it, let's simply it further: username on line 1, password
> on line 2. Then there's no ambiguity regarding the position in the line.

Done in v10. mail-creds now holds the login on line 1 and the password
on line 2. remotes.env and mbsyncrc both read each line whole with
sed -n 1p and sed -n 2p, and the spec and the README, with its printf
example, describe the new format. The series is "[PATCH v10 0/6]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay jail" [1].

> Still, it needs to verify that both username and password are present.

remotes.env reads both lines and calls rc.subr's err if either is
empty, so smtprelay refuses to start and names the file:

    ERROR: /data/secrets/mail-creds needs the login on line 1 and
    the password on line 2

I sourced it here after /etc/rc.subr, under set -a as rc.subr does.
It fails like that for a missing file, an empty file, one line only,
and an empty first or second line, and builds the right remote from
a good file. That much is fact. I have not run it inside lab-mail.

Two things you may want to weigh. rc.subr sources the env file for
every smtprelay command, so with a broken mail-creds, stop and status
fail too until the file is fixed. A check on rc_arg would limit it to
start if you prefer. mbsync does no check of its own. With a missing
line it sends an empty login or password, and I expect Fastmail to
refuse it and the fetch log to show it, but that is inference.

Thanks.

